for both of you uh there's been at least  four documentaries i think uh how does  that fit into  the decision decision process to take on  the the subject  and the approach funnily enough when i  optioned the book way back when in 2004  i  was introduced to the book i and i had  no idea that the documentaries existed  and i'll never forget the day after i  optioned the book that i learned  the documentaries existed  and um so i was kind of you know like  taken aback a little bit but my  introduction to the to the story was  through the book  and i'll never forget the day that i  put the two documentaries in and watch  them back to back for about four  straight hours and just sat there with  my jaw dropped  and because i don't know if any of you  have seen the documentaries but they're  quite um  it's quite a colorful cast of characters  and  it  it  both intrigued me further but it also  was very interesting because to be quite  honest i had  a reaction to damien  that i think was probably  i could understand  why the town  was able to be manipulated  and and and whipped up into this frenzy  because damien  is  a very interesting very bright  um and and complicated  unique  personality very strong personality and  so i it kind of set me on a journey  um  of doing even more due diligence on on  the project and and and kind of just  reassuring myself that that it was the  right thing to make the film  um and uh but that's that's the impact  it had  it's interesting that the the  documentaries actually did really focus  on  a tiny slice of the story and and the  book  um  was really a much bigger picture um  and uh going in we knew that the world  had sort of seen these documentaries so  we were going to have to find new angles  and new information and and there is a  lot of  new information in this film that that  never came out in any of those  documentaries and  we just thought it was important to show  other sides to this story because it's  such a complex story that has so many  different people involved we chose two  characters that had very little  spotlight on them prior to us making  this film we had um  a couple of directors that  were attached well one in particular was  attached prior to adam  and um  but then adam was really  when we found adam it kind of unlocked  the  the the whole  project and and thrust it immediately  forward and i think that i think with  every film it's funny you go through all  these journeys but i think you always  end up exactly where you're supposed to  end up well reece was always  our first choice and we were very  fortunate to get her  and um  colin i'm sorry i mean well colin and  adam had worked together once before and  i think um adam adam really felt the  same thing about colin which is that he  was his first choice and  um they had a working relationship and  we just felt that you know ron lax  is is a real person who unfortunately  recently passed away actually since we  completed the film  um but uh he was this kind of  strong quiet individual i mean liz liz  had a you know a close relationship with  him and knew knew him quite well and we  really felt that that colin  embodied that the spirit of this of this  individual he did and ron it was funny  we showed the film to ron and his wife  and  and uh it was not conscious on colin's  part at all  um but  his wife felt that ron had i mean that  colin had absolutely just captured ron  down to his walk but that was all um  very organic it was not you know a  conscious mimicry or anything we've  become so accustomed to seeing southern  courtrooms i was thinking as i was  watching the film you know there's  legacy all the way back clearly to kill  a mockingbird and movies like that but  also the grisham  based films uh  and uh do you think that that heritage  is a a challenge for a movie like that  like this or an opportunity because  people understand the rhythms and the  and the  the tropes of it or do you think that  you kind of have to  separate from all the melodrama  well i think that um one thing that we  were very conscious of just in terms of  it  taking place in the south  and  this being a story about real people  from the south  we were  we were very conscious about wanting to  be certain that we portrayed them  not stereotypically in any way which is  obviously  a kind of pitfall that you can  fall into and i think that adam did a  fabulous job with that and all of our  actors we were all very conscious of  that and  i'm really proud of the way it turned  out from that from that aspect jason  baldwin one of the west memphis three  is an executive producer um with us  again liz liz really spent a lot of time  um  meeting and knowing these characters  like pam hobbs and and jason and other  you know players who were involved in  the case and so  jason was  he had always really liked the book and  and in fact met his fiancee  through the author of the book um and so  uh  he he came on board as one of our  executive producers so we had  really  created jessie  as did as a jesse miss kelly um and pam  hobbs was very involved um  ron lax so we really had a great uh  uh cadre of people to advise us because  they were the the real people who were  involved in and and jason baldwin uh  came to the set a few days and in fact  he came to the set when we were filming  the courtroom sequences and he just said  it was the most surreal thing for him to  sort of go back and see himself be be  sentenced to life in prison once again  in this courtroom um but we again we  felt really blessed to have people such  as himself so closely attached to our  project adam also  made a decision early on  because this was a story that had been  told through some documentaries so  people had seen  some of the real places and some of the  real people  you know he decided that he wasn't  trying to replicate everything he was  really trying to you know find the  essence of the story and tell it in his  own way and in the courtroom is is just  a good example of that which is that the  actual courtroom was a very modern  boring courtroom but you know we found  this location that was kind of more of a  classic southern  old courtroom uh that had great light  coming through the windows and that kind  of thing and  again he said that was that was the  right thing to do because he wasn't  trying to simply  recreate every last detail that people  had already seen in the documentaries oh  that's interesting because because you  could really go sort of the in cold  blood route where you know you're trying  to do as as close to journalism as  possible or mississippi burning where  you kind of take the the mythology  and  in in contrast to what adam said our our  writers  actually determined early on that they  really wanted to  try to be as close as possible to the  story and to the facts and so  the the script itself is very very  factual and was based on a lot of core  transcripts and so there's it's not like  there's a lot of drop you know of  fictionalizing of the story but in the  way adam wanted to approach it again he  wasn't just trying to make another  documentary
 

